From: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scsi: ufs: qcom: only take the lane sync clocks in the variant

ufs_qcom_init_lane_clks() calls devm_clk_bulk_get_all(), which takes *every*
clock in the ufshc node -- core_clk, bus_aggr_clk, iface_clk, core_clk_unipro,
ref_clk and ice_core_clk as well as the three lane sync clocks. Those are the
same clocks the ufshcd core already owns: ufshcd_parse_operating_points()
builds hba->clk_list from the identical clock-names, and ufshcd_setup_clocks()
enables and gates them.

So every non-lane clock is managed twice, by the core and by the variant, with
neither aware of the other's reference. The variant's reference is never
dropped at idle, so gcc_ufs_phy_* stay enabled, gpll0 and thence the rpmhcc XO
stay up, clk-rpmh writes xo.lvl = 0x3 into the RPMH sleep set, and qcom_stats
aosd/cxsd stay pinned at 0. Measured on a OnePlus 6 (sdm845): enable_count on
gcc_ufs_phy_{rx,tx}_symbol_* and gcc_ufs_phy_ice_core_clk climbs 1 -> 5 over
four forced runtime suspend/resume cycles and never returns to 0.

Trying to balance that count inside the variant does not work, because it is not
the variant's count to balance -- both directions were built and boot-tested and
both hang the controller at power/runtime_status "resuming" with every LUN queue
frozen: guarding ufs_qcom_enable_lane_clks() makes a needed enable a no-op, and
letting the second disable through drops the clocks while the link still needs
them to exit hibern8.

Take only the lane sync clocks in the variant, which is the split the downstream
driver uses (it holds rx/tx_lane[01]_sync_clk and nothing else), and leave the
rest to the core so its clock gating can actually take them to zero.

Signed-off-by: Maximilian Gaedig <sly-keg-cheating@duck.com>
---
--- a/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/ufs/host/ufs-qcom.c
@@ -373,19 +373,40 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Only the lane symbol clocks belong to the variant. Everything else in the
+ * ufshc node is already on hba->clk_list and is enabled/gated by the ufshcd
+ * core; taking those here too gives them a second, never-released reference
+ * and pins the rpmhcc XO. Matches the downstream driver's split.
+ */
+static const char * const ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names[] = {
+	"tx_lane0_sync_clk",
+	"rx_lane0_sync_clk",
+	"rx_lane1_sync_clk",
+};
+
 static int ufs_qcom_init_lane_clks(struct ufs_qcom_host *host)
 {
-	int err;
 	struct device *dev = host->hba->dev;
+	int i, err;
 
 	if (has_acpi_companion(dev))
 		return 0;
 
-	err = devm_clk_bulk_get_all(dev, &host->clks);
-	if (err <= 0)
+	host->clks = devm_kcalloc(dev, ARRAY_SIZE(ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names),
+				  sizeof(*host->clks), GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!host->clks)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names); i++)
+		host->clks[i].id = ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names[i];
+
+	err = devm_clk_bulk_get_optional(dev,
+			ARRAY_SIZE(ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names), host->clks);
+	if (err)
 		return err;
 
-	host->num_clks = err;
+	host->num_clks = ARRAY_SIZE(ufs_qcom_lane_clk_names);
 
 	return 0;
 }
